{"kind":"report","text":"approved at exact clean head 7d6211540692bee2a5b9415389c2f9794112137a, artifact e04566e92c8e7f3120de16e8491c8c9ff8255d89. No findings.\n\nThe finding is closed at the right place. decideRatify now refuses with \"operator standing is required to ratify an operator grant\" when the target grant names `operator` and the ratifier does not hold it. That is the creation side of the boundary, which is where the asymmetry was: removal already demanded operator standing, and creation did not, so minting a new operator was the cheap way around a rule about retiring one. Three lines, and they close it at the door rather than by adding a compensating check elsewhere.\n\nI re-attacked rather than confirming, because my own finding prompted this change and I am the reviewer most likely to accept a fix that closes the path I described while leaving its neighbours open. Four routes, all closed:\n\nThe two-actor mint. A ratifier authoring an operator grant for a confederate and ratifying it is now refused at the ratification. Covered by TestRatifierCannotMintOperatorForAnotherActor.\n\nRatifier proliferation. A ratifier can still mint another ratifier — the satisfier for a non-operator roster grant is unchanged — but that no longer escalates, because no number of ratifiers can ratify an operator grant. The proliferation is real and harmless.\n\nIndirect ratification. There is no chain to exploit: a ratify must rest on exactly its target, and decideRatify refuses any target that is not a statement, so the new check inspects the actual grant rather than something that resolves to one.\n\nRestoration as a way in. Superseding a supersession to revive a dormant operator grant still requires operator standing, through governanceTarget's alternating restoring flag and membershipCarriesOperator. A ratifier cannot reach an operator grant by reversing its retirement either.\n\nOne consequence worth stating rather than treating as a defect: an operator cannot renew or re-grant their own authority, because the beneficiary rule blocks self-authored and self-ratified grants regardless of standing. That is more restrictive than strictly required, and it is the right direction for a governance boundary — it just means a sole operator cannot restore themselves unaided, which is worth knowing before it happens rather than during.\n\nDocumentation is accurate and artifact-backed. Both ratify pages now state the target-class rule — `operator` for an operator grant, otherwise `ratifier` — and rest on artifact de8f9a5e, which is live at path `.` for commit cc32f82c.\n\nTests: go vet clean; internal/workroom passes at this head with the new case. The head contains current main 26c9bb3b and is the tip of its branch. I did not run the full suite; the behaviour lives in internal/workroom and the rest of the change is documentation, but run it before merge.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:e04566e92c8e7f3120de16e8491c8c9ff8255d89","branch":"request/governance-authority-repair","head":"7d6211540692bee2a5b9415389c2f9794112137a","tests":"go vet clean; internal/workroom passes at this head including TestRatifierCannotMintOperatorForAnotherActor; full suite not run","verdict":"approved"}}